                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="explain" value="&lt;b&gt;Why diagonal banking?&lt;/b&gt;&#xa;&#xa;A vector register holds a 2D image grid (8×4 shown; 128×128 in hardware). Its pixels are spread across 8 SRAM banks by the rule &lt;b&gt;bank = (row + col) mod 8&lt;/b&gt;.&#xa;&#xa;Because every pixel on one anti-diagonal (constant row+col) maps to the same bank, any full &lt;i&gt;row&lt;/i&gt;, any full &lt;i&gt;column&lt;/i&gt;, or any &lt;i&gt;diagonal&lt;/i&gt; of the grid touches each of the 8 banks at most once. The hardware can therefore read all those access patterns in parallel with &lt;b&gt;no bank conflicts&lt;/b&gt; — the key to transposing / sliding the image cheaply." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FAFAFA;strokeColor=#9E9E9E;strokeWidth=1;dashed=1;fontSize=13;fontColor=#212121;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;spacingRight=12;" parent="1" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="160" y="640" width="700" height="170" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="2" value="VRF Diagonal Banking" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=22;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#212121;" parent="1" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="60" y="880" width="700" height="30" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="3" value="Each pixel (row, col) of a register lives in bank = (row + col) mod 8. Pixels on the same anti-diagonal share a bank, giving the colour stripes." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=13;fontColor=#616161;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="60" y="912" width="780" height="20" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="52" value="× 32 vector registers (v0 … v31)&#xa;each register is one image grid;&#xa;all share the same bank layout" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=12;fontColor=#546E7A;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="628" y="1600" width="240" height="60" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="53" style="endArrow=open;html=1;rounded=0;strokeColor=#546E7A;strokeWidth=1.5;dashed=1;exitX=0.3;exitY=0;entryX=0.8;entryY=1;" parent="1" source="52" target="4" edge="1">
                    <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="63" value="&lt;b&gt;Why diagonal banking?&lt;/b&gt;&#xa;&#xa;A vector register holds a 2D image grid (8×4 shown; 128×128 in hardware). Its pixels are spread across 8 SRAM banks by the rule &lt;b&gt;bank = (row + col) mod 8&lt;/b&gt;.&#xa;&#xa;Because every pixel on one anti-diagonal (constant row+col) maps to the same bank, any full &lt;i&gt;row&lt;/i&gt;, any full &lt;i&gt;column&lt;/i&gt;, or any &lt;i&gt;diagonal&lt;/i&gt; of the grid touches each of the 8 banks at most once. The hardware can therefore read all those access patterns in parallel with &lt;b&gt;no bank conflicts&lt;/b&gt; — the key to transposing / sliding the image cheaply." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FAFAFA;strokeColor=#9E9E9E;strokeWidth=1;dashed=1;fontSize=13;fontColor=#212121;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;spacingRight=12;" parent="1" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="160" y="1660" width="700" height="170" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="65" value="" style="group" parent="1" vertex="1" connectable="0">
                    <mxGeometry x="1050" y="1060" width="240" height="364" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="54" value="VRF banks (8 physical SRAM banks)" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=15;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#212121;" parent="65" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry width="240" height="22" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
