                <mxCell id="141" value="&lt;b&gt;Why diagonal banking?&lt;/b&gt;&#xa;&#xa;A vector register holds a 2D image grid (8×4 shown; 128×128 in hardware). Its pixels are spread across 8 SRAM banks by the rule &lt;b&gt;bank = (row + col) mod 8&lt;/b&gt;.&#xa;&#xa;Because every pixel on one anti-diagonal (constant row+col) maps to the same bank, any full &lt;i&gt;row&lt;/i&gt;, any full &lt;i&gt;column&lt;/i&gt;, or any &lt;i&gt;diagonal&lt;/i&gt; of the grid touches each of the 8 banks at most once. The hardware can therefore read all those access patterns in parallel with &lt;b&gt;no bank conflicts&lt;/b&gt; — the key to transposing / sliding the image cheaply." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FAFAFA;strokeColor=#9E9E9E;strokeWidth=1;dashed=1;fontSize=13;fontColor=#212121;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;spacingRight=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="1532" y="1017.37" width="700" height="170" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
